Dockerfile Commands – Story Style 


Imagine you're setting up a mini food truck – lightweight, clean, just what’s needed.
This is exactly how a Docker container works.

Let’s break down each Dockerfile instruction with this story:
WORKDIR – Set your place
Before starting, you decide where you’ll work inside your truck.
So you say:
      WORKDIR /app
Now all your tasks will happen in /app – no more cd commands every time.
 It’s like saying: “Put all ingredients here, I’ll cook from this shelf.”


COPY vs ADD – Bring the stuff in
You want to bring your ingredients (code/files) into the truck.
      COPY . /app
It simply places your local files into the container.

Now let’s say you have a zipped recipe or need to fetch ingredients from a URL?
Use:
      ADD recipes.zip /app
ADD = COPY + more powers (unzip, download from URL).
But unless you need those powers, always prefer COPY.


CMD vs ENTRYPOINT – What to run?
You’ve set up everything.
Now, when the truck starts, what should it do?
     ENTRYPOINT ["python"]
     CMD ["app.py"]
Think of it like this:
ENTRYPOINT is the fixed engine — your container always starts with this.
CMD is the default gear — it can change if someone wants to override it.
Together, this runs: python app.py
If someone overrides CMD, it becomes: python test.py
 But ENTRYPOINT (python) stays the same.
Use ENTRYPOINT when you always want a command to run (like a fixed engine).
Use CMD for flexible arguments.



Multistage Build – Keep it lean
Imagine cooking with 10 tools – knives, grinders, etc.
But when delivering the food, you only pack the dish, not all the tools.
That’s Multistage Build!
      # Stage 1: Build
          FROM node:20 as builder
          WORKDIR /app
          COPY . .
          RUN npm install && npm run build

       # Stage 2: Final container
           FROM nginx:alpine
           COPY --from=builder /app/dist /usr/share/nginx/html
Stage 1 builds your app.
Stage 2 only copies the final product – clean and optimized.



In an interview, just say this:
FROM sets the base image.
WORKDIR sets the working folder.
COPY and ADD bring files inside.
ENTRYPOINT runs a fixed command, CMD gives a default argument.
And Multistage Builds help us keep the final image small and fast.”
If this helped you, imagine what CMD, ENTRYPOINT, COPY, ADD, and WORKDIR can do in real-world projects 
 
